{"_e":"metro:client_log","_t":1786126612732,"level":"log","data":["Response body:","{\"answer_id\":59,\"total_score\":3,\"scores\":{\"situation\":3,\"task\":0,\"action\":0,\"result\":0},\"strengths\":[\"The candidate attempted to address the question.\"],\"improvements\":[\"The response is extremely vague and lacks any context or detail about the situation. It doesn't explain *who* was struggling, *what* they were struggling with, or *why* treats were chosen as a motivational tool.\"],\"feedback\":\"This answer fails to demonstrate an understanding of effective motivation techniques. It’s a very simplistic response that doesn’t provide any insight into the candidate’s leadership skills or ability to address performance issues.\",\"suggested_answer\":\"Let me tell you about a time when Sarah, a junior designer on my team, was consistently missing deadlines and producing work below our quality standards. I recognized she seemed hesitant to ask for help, so I scheduled a one-on-one meeting where we openly discussed her challenges – she was feeling overwhelmed with the new software and unsure of how to prioritize tasks effectively. I then worked with her to create a detailed project plan with smaller, more manageable milestones, paired her with a senior designer for mentorship, and provided regular feedback focused on celebrating small wins. To measure improvement, we tracked her task completion rate, quality scores on design reviews, and her self-reported confidence levels over the following two weeks. Her completion rate increased by 20%, her quality scores improved by 15%, and she reported feeling significantly more confident in her abilities.\"}"]}
