{"_e":"metro:client_log","_t":1786238045772,"level":"log","data":["Response body:","{\"answer_id\":101,\"total_score\":0,\"scores\":{\"situation\":0,\"task\":0,\"action\":0,\"result\":0},\"strengths\":[\"The candidate attempted to answer the question.\"],\"improvements\":[\"The response is completely insufficient. The candidate did not provide any context or details about the situation, task, action, or result.\",\"The candidate needs to demonstrate an understanding of how to structure a STAR response.\"],\"feedback\":\"This response demonstrates a fundamental misunderstanding of behavioral interview techniques. It’s crucial for candidates to articulate their experiences using the STAR method to effectively showcase their skills and problem-solving abilities. Without any information, it's impossible to evaluate the candidate's capabilities.\",\"suggested_answer\":\"\\\"Certainly. In my previous role at [Company Name], we were using a model to predict customer churn. Initially, the model had a relatively high false positive rate – flagging many customers who weren’t actually going to leave. (Situation) My task was to investigate this and identify ways to improve its accuracy. I began by analyzing the feature importance scores from the model, which highlighted that engagement metrics like website visits and app usage were significantly contributing to the false positives. (Task) I proposed a change to the model’s weighting of these features, reducing their influence slightly and increasing the weight on demographic factors that were more strongly correlated with actual churn. I also suggested implementing a rule-based system to flag customers who met specific criteria – low engagement combined with recent negative customer service interactions. (Action) To measure the impact, we A/B tested the revised model against the original for a two-week period. We tracked key metrics like precision and recall, as well as the number of actual churned customers. The revised model showed a 15% improvement in precision – meaning fewer false positives – while maintaining similar recall rates. This resulted in a reduction of approximately 8% in wasted marketing spend targeting at-risk customers. (Result)\\\"\"}"]}
{"_e":"metro:client_log","_t":1786238045773,"level":"log","data":["STAR evaluation:","{\"answer_id\": 101, \"feedback\": \"This response demonstrates a fundamental misunderstanding of behavioral interview techniques. It’s crucial for candidates to articulate their experiences using the STAR method to effectively showcase their skills and problem-solving abilities. Without any information, it's impossible to evaluate the candidate's capabilities.\", \"improvements\": [\"The response is completely insufficient. The candidate did not provide any context or details about the situation, task, action, or result.\", \"The candidate needs to demonstrate an understanding of how to structure a STAR response.\"], \"scores\": {\"action\": 0, \"result\": 0, \"situation\": 0, \"task\": 0}, \"strengths\": [\"The candidate attempted to answer the question.\"], \"suggested_answer\": \"\\\"Certainly. In my previous role at [Company Name], we were using a model to predict customer churn. Initially, the model had a relatively high false positive rate – flagging many customers who weren’t actually going to leave. (Situation) My task was to investigate this and identify ways to improve its accuracy. I began by analyzing the feature importance scores from the model, which highlighted that engagement metrics like website visits and app usage were significantly contributing to the false positives. (Task) I proposed a change to the model’s weighting of these features, reducing their influence slightly and increasing the weight on demographic factors that were more strongly correlated with actual churn. I also suggested implementing a rule-based system to flag customers who met specific criteria – low engagement combined with recent negative customer service interactions. (Action) To measure the impact, we A/B tested the revised model against the original for a two-week period. We tracked key metrics like precision and recall, as well as the number of actual churned customers. The revised model showed a 15% improvement in precision – meaning fewer false positives – while maintaining similar recall rates. This resulted in a reduction of approximately 8% in wasted marketing spend targeting at-risk customers. (Result)\\\"\", \"total_score\": 0}"]}
